title='Supplementary Material'
q=p(title,'paper title');q.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER
p('Recovery–Retention Trade-offs in Adapting a Pretrained Dexterous Grasp Planner','Heading 1')
p('This supplement records implementation details and secondary analyses of the existing experiments. The main manuscript contains the recovery–retention comparison, lock-posture tests and compact-correction results.')

h('S1. IMPLEMENTATION AND EVALUATION DETAILS')
table('TABLE S1. PHASES AND OPTIMIZER CALLS', ['Procedure','Phases','Iterations','Epochs','Mini-batches','Adam calls','Attempts'],
      [[r['method'],r['phases'],r['iterations'],r['epochs'],r['minibatches'],r['adam_steps'],f"{r['attempts']:,}"] for r in S['optimizer_calls']],
      [2.03,.55,.73,.61,.88,.78,1.66],
      'Iterations sum across phases; calls = iterations × epochs × minibatches. A call is counted even for a group whose normalized rewards produce zero policy gradient. These are the recorded training schedules, not equal-compute claims.')
p('A phase uses a fixed object list and initializes policy weights from the preceding phase. Full-weight PPO uses phase iterations 26, 26 and 25 with 256 environments. Full-weight GRPO uses 12 phases totaling 38 iterations with 512 environments; residual procedures total 37 iterations. The four primary procedures therefore use 19,712, 19,456, 18,944 and 18,944 interactions, respectively. The full-weight procedures receive 4.1% and 2.7% more interactions than residual procedures.')
p('Full-weight PPO jointly optimizes actor, critic and their shared PointNet backbone with Adam. Its loss includes the clipped policy surrogate and 2.0 times the value loss; global gradient clipping follows backpropagation. Residual PPO uses a frozen PointNet embedding and concatenated non-point-cloud observation; its correction and value networks share Adam, the selected learning rate and global clipping, with a 0.5 value-loss coefficient. The residual program also contains exploratory SAC/DSRL options; they are not used for the reported PPO/GRPO comparisons. No critic-gradient detachment control was run.')
p('The selected residual procedures learn ρ and use five epochs. Matched residual GRPO fixes scale to max(σ₀, 0.05), freezes ρ and uses two epochs. The matched full-weight GRPO reference is a separately trained set of runs, distinct from the original selected full-weight models. The added matched rate is fixed at 3×10⁻⁴; there is no outcome-driven search for further rates.')
p('The released observation interface omits joint state, fault labels and execution history. Planner features concatenate the non-point-cloud observation with the frozen PointNet point-cloud embedding. A single selected action is executed for the grasp episode and scored by the binary lift outcome. PCL adds Gaussian noise to point-cloud coordinates only; the object-pose observation is unchanged. The unseen-category list has 100 slots for 78 unique objects; 22 objects occur twice. Success is averaged within each seen/unseen list and those two rates receive equal weight within each training run.')
p('The collision filter disables robot–table physical contact in half the parallel environments. Palm/fingertip geometric checks remain active everywhere. The collision-enabled check changes both robot–table contact and the ground plane. Original selected models and new full-weight candidates are reevaluated against same-server frozen references for each new comparison. First-episode state hashes match; a small number of later reset/settling states differ. All outcomes are retained and inference uses independent training runs.')
p('Friction arguments are retained exactly in the public command manifest: full-weight PPO uses --mus 1.0; other training/evaluation programs use --mus none. The code applies these settings through its program-specific environment wrapper. The archived PPO readback confirms the requested override; there is no new physical-property readback in this revision. The simulation and training scripts remain unchanged by the manuscript edits.')

h('S2. GRID EXTENSION AND SECONDARY CHECKS')
p('The extension starts with 18 runs and permits at most 26. For either full-weight family, an initial three-run development mean loss in the closed interval [4, 6] pp in either LF or PCL triggers two added runs in both conditions, once. PPO does not trigger; GRPO does, giving 22 completed runs and 426,496 training interactions. The three evaluation batches contain 361, 146 and 36 formal units, totaling 543 units and 565,072 attempts. Frozen development calibration adds a separate 2,048 attempts. Development selection was written before test-effectiveness analysis.')
orig=json.loads((R.parent/'cscwd2027_v4/table_audit.json').read_text(encoding='utf8'))
t3=orig['TABLE III. SAME-RUNTIME EXTENSION RESULTS']
table('TABLE S2. SAME-SERVER EXTENSION CONFIGURATIONS',t3['headers'],t3['rows'],[2.9,1.35,1.50,1.49],
      'Success (%) and nominal loss (pp, parentheses); n is runs per training condition. Frozen nominal success is 91.3%. The last two rows have fixed scale and two epochs. Matched residual 3×10⁻⁴ fails development feasibility at 7.8125 pp LF loss; full-weight GRPO 2×10⁻⁵ fails at 5.64453125 pp PCL loss.')
p('For 40 identical archived checkpoints, development nominal loss changes by an average of 1.195068359375 pp on the second GPU server; the frozen score changes by 1.220703125 pp. No original-grid candidate changes feasibility. PPO’s selected rate changes because 2×10⁻⁵ is added, while GRPO keeps 10⁻⁵. This separates environmental reevaluation from the grid-extension effect.')
p('The original LF-only selection gives a 23.5 pp averaged reference-LF contrast; a shared ten-point nominal-loss limit gives 18.0 pp. These are alternative selection analyses of existing candidate configurations. Full-weight PPO 3×10⁻⁵ misses the shared five-point rule by 0.20 pp on PCL; full-weight GRPO at that rate has 8.45 pp LF development loss.')
p('A KL-penalized full-weight GRPO baseline achieves 36.8% reference-LF success with 2.4 pp nominal loss. The selected rate is 3×10⁻⁵ and penalty coefficient is 0.00528. Coefficients are calibrated as {0.1, 1}/K̄, where K̄ is the mean Gaussian mean-shift KL diagnostic over six selected residual-GRPO checkpoints on a common nominal-state set. Rate and coefficient are selected jointly on development LF/PCL. This is a tested KL grid rather than an exhaustive retention-aware baseline search.')
p('Ideal fault routing sends nominal episodes to the frozen planner and removes the retention constraint when selecting fault-specific configurations. It gives a 14.5 [2.2, 26.9] pp averaged residual contrast (Holm p = 0.048). The routing signal is supplied by the evaluation; no deployed fault detector is evaluated. Enabling all robot–table collisions and adding a ground plane changes the original LF procedure contrast by 0.2 [−0.9, 1.4] pp. Both checks retain their original analysis scope.')

h('S3. ORIGINAL DIRECTIONAL TESTS')
t4=orig['TABLE IV. ORIGINAL NINE DIRECTIONAL TESTS']
table('TABLE S3. THE ORIGINAL NINE-TEST FAMILY',t4['headers'],t4['rows'],[.65,2.7,2.6,1.29],
      'Effects and two-sided unadjusted 95% Welch intervals are in pp. Decisions use one-sided p-values with Holm correction across these nine tests. H4 labels show mean gain over frozen before → after combining shifts; its effect is the reduction in that gain. All nine effects, intervals and p-values are unchanged.')
p('H1 compares update procedures at reference LF; H2 compares their LF and PCL contrasts. H3 tests alternative 25% and 75% test-time lock postures. H4 reuses models trained on a single shift when adding a second evaluation shift. H5 evaluates ideal fault routing. The curve interpolation and bootstrap in S4 are descriptive additions and are not included in this family.')

h('S4. OBSERVED CURVES AND UNCERTAINTY')
z=S['interpolation'];a=z['estimate']
p(f'The descriptive curve statistic is the equal-optimizer mean of residual minus full-weight success, averaged uniformly over the nominal-loss range shared by all four observed test curves. The original curves share [{a["lower"]:.6f}, {a["upper"]:.6f}] pp. Within each arm, points are sorted by observed mean nominal loss and connected piecewise linearly; the integral is computed over the union of all breakpoints. No curve is extrapolated. The resulting gap is {a["effect"]:.6f} pp.')
p(f'Bootstrap seed {z["bootstrap_seed"]} and {z["bootstrap_draws"]:,} draws are fixed for this analysis. Within each rate, an independent training run is resampled as a paired fault/nominal observation. Each draw recomputes the four means, their common support and the integrated mean gap; {z["valid_draws"]:,} draws have nonempty support. Candidate means with exactly equal nominal costs are averaged before interpolation. The percentile interval is [{z["percentile_CI95"][0]:.6f}, {z["percentile_CI95"][1]:.6f}] pp. This estimates uncertainty in a curve functional of the recorded grid, not the performance of a newly trained equal-retention policy.')
p('The directly observed PPO comparison is 50.2% success at 5.1 pp loss for full-weight PPO versus 65.2% at 5.4 pp for residual PPO. GRPO also has observed high-recovery points at roughly 7.7/8.2 pp loss, both beyond the original development limit. These points contextualize selection sensitivity; the original and expanded development-selected results remain separate.')
table('TABLE S4. SAME-SERVER RUN-LEVEL INTERVALS', ['Procedure','Condition','n','Success [95% CI]','Loss [95% CI]'],
      [[{'fwppo':'Full PPO','fwgrpo':'Full GRPO','res_ppo':'Residual PPO','res_grpo':'Residual GRPO'}[r['method']],('PCL' if r['condition']=='pn10' else r['condition']),r['loss']['n'],statfmt(r['success']),statfmt(r['loss'])] for r in S['same_runtime_selected']],
      [1.5,.7,.35,2.35,2.34],
      'Intervals are two-sided Student intervals over training runs; the frozen nominal reference is treated as fixed. A negative lower loss bound is retained because nominal performance can improve. These are operating-point intervals; cross-procedure contrasts use Welch–Satterthwaite calculations.')

h('S5. COMPACT CORRECTIONS AND INTERPRETATION')
p('Post-training constants average a residual network’s correction over saved nominal training-object states, with no further optimizer updates. The source network and extracted constant share training runs, so their differences are paired. A directly trained constant plus scale has 48 parameters and selects on LF. Raw/scaled mean-only shifts each have 24 parameters, fixed sampling scale and joint LF/PCL selection. All constant controls use the frozen planner for state-dependent grasp proposals.')
p('Equation (3) establishes representational equivalence between a constant mean shift and an output-bias change. For Adam, d = σ₀ ⊙ c gives g_c = σ₀ ⊙ g_d; with matched initialization and negligible numerical stabilizer, effective steps in d approximately scale with σ₀. This coordinate argument does not equate a frozen mean-only update with full-network optimization, which also changes features and distributes gradients across layers. The reported checkpoint diagnostics are descriptive. No RFJ3-only intervention, mixed full-network/scaled-bias training or detached-critic PPO control was run in this revision.')
p('At the reference lock, the extracted mean has a paired success difference of 2.4 [−6.2, 11.1] pp from its source network. Thus the recorded high recovery can be reproduced by a common shift; the interval does not establish superiority or formal equivalence. At 75% retraining and two-finger lock, state-dependent corrections retain higher point estimates. These distinct fault configurations test the range of the compact baseline.')

h('S6. REPRODUCTION')
p('The public repository retains the original October records and adds extensions/cscwd2027_1102 for the completed extension. The CPU recompute script reads ordered official per-attempt binary flags, rebuilds development selection, 150 run-condition scores and 16 descriptive contrasts, and checks them against the release record. Manifests map source file hashes, object lists, training phases, rates and seeds. The manuscript and plotting builders read the two numerical ledgers; no table numbers are entered in Word by hand.')
p('The numeric-validation fix keeps counts, strings, keys and list lengths exact, while accepting float roundoff at absolute tolerance 10⁻⁸ and relative tolerance 10⁻¹⁰. A 1.5×10⁻¹⁰ quantile perturbation passes; a 10⁻⁴ pp change fails. No reference values are rounded or changed to make this check pass. The main manuscript remains self-contained; this supplement supplies the implementation and secondary evidence.')
